Delegation succeeded after the -Confirm:$false fix. LastTaskResult: 0. Transcript ran clean
through all three cmdlets to === DONE ===, no stall.

Root cause of the earlier stall, confirmed correct by this success: PowerShell's default
$ConfirmPreference (High) triggers an interactive ShouldProcess confirmation for these cmdlets;
under the scheduled task's non-interactive session that prompt had no console to read from and
blocked indefinitely. -Confirm:$false on all three calls removed the prompt entirely. Isolating
Get-ADForest beforehand (0.13s) had already ruled out DNS/GC/enumeration slowness as the cause.

CONFIRMED IN THE LIVE ACL (not inferred from clean exit alone) - dsacls "DC=district,DC=local"
via the transcript's own ACE dump, before and after PasswordHashSync ran:

  After BasicRead + MsDsConsistencyGuid only:
    svc-entraconnect has SPECIAL ACCESS / READ PROPERTY on subobject types (user, group,
    computer, contact, device, foreignSecurityPrincipal, inetOrgPerson) and, after
    MsDsConsistencyGuid, WRITE PROPERTY / READ PROPERTY on mS-DS-ConsistencyGuid specifically.
    No replication rights at this point.

  After PasswordHashSync ran, new domain-root-level entries appeared:
    Allow DISTRICT\svc-entraconnect       Replicating Directory Changes
    Allow DISTRICT\svc-entraconnect       Replicating Directory Changes All

  This is the DCSync-equivalent grant, confirmed present by direct ACL comparison across the
  three stages rather than assumed from "the cmdlet didn't error."

Each cmdlet's own dsacls-style output ended with:
  "The command completed successfully"
  "WARNING: You will need to perform a full import to include any objects that may have come
   in scope as a result of this operation."
(standard AdSyncConfig output, not an error - noting because "WARNING" reads alarming out of
context)

Set-ADSyncRestrictedPermissions RUN AND VERIFIED, next. Run interactively at VM102's console
(not the scheduled task - this cmdlet takes -Credential directly):

  $cred = Get-Credential -UserName "DISTRICT\Administrator" -Message "..."
  Import-Module "...\AdSyncConfig.psm1"
  Set-ADSyncRestrictedPermissions -ADConnectorAccountDN "CN=svc-entraconnect,OU=Service Accounts,DC=district,DC=local" -Credential $cred -Confirm:$false

Console output was a single line - "Set-ADSyncRestrictedPermissions: Setting Restricted
permissions on '...'..." - then a clean prompt, no error, no completion message. Ambiguous on
its own (unlike the other three cmdlets, this one produced no ACL dump / "command completed
successfully" line), so verified against the live object rather than trusted:

  Import-Module ActiveDirectory
  $acl = Get-Acl -Path "AD:\CN=svc-entraconnect,OU=Service Accounts,DC=district,DC=local"
  $acl.AreAccessRulesProtected  ->  True
  $acl.Access | Select IdentityReference,AccessControlType,ActiveDirectoryRights

  AreAccessRulesProtected: True confirms inheritance from OU=Service Accounts is now blocked -
  the actual mechanism behind "restricted permissions." Remaining explicit ACL is tight and
  correct: Domain Admins / Enterprise Admins / SYSTEM / BUILTIN\Administrators at GenericAll,
  SELF with narrow ReadProperty/WriteProperty + ExtendedRight on its own attributes,
  ENTERPRISE DOMAIN CONTROLLERS and Authenticated Users at GenericRead. No stray delegated
  access carried over from the OU.

  Minor procedural note: the first verification attempt failed with "Cannot find drive. A
  drive with the name 'AD' does not exist" - the AD: PSDrive only exists after
  Import-Module ActiveDirectory, which the first call omitted. Not a finding, just a gap in
  the command as first written.

CONNECTOR ACCOUNT PROVISIONING IS COMPLETE. svc-entraconnect: no admin group membership, in
Service Accounts OU, basic read + ConsistencyGuid + PHS delegated at the domain root, own
object ACL hardened against inheritance. Only remaining step in this whole thread: fully exit
and relaunch the Entra Connect wizard on VM102, now genuinely domain-joined with a fully
permissioned connector account - the actual goal of this exercise, and the second test of the
"stale wizard state" hypothesis from entra-connect-install.
